= 1932

@intro_to_sask

Saskatchewan seems to be one of the hardest to understand provinces in Canada. In the last one hundred years, it has alternated between the staunchly conservative right-wing economics of the Conservative, Liberal, and Saskatchewan Party. Most dominant however, was the CCF/NDP, a Social Democratic Party in a sea of rural voters. It elected the government of Tommy Douglas who Canadians know is famous for introducing Universal Healthcare. It elected Allan Blakeney who directed government ownership in the province's prestigious potash reserves. Yet it also elected the government of Grant Devine, a follower of Reaganomics who sent much of the provincial welfare state and crown corporations into deep depths underground. 

This picture becomes clearer when we paint the colourful picture of Saskatchewan history.

At its inauguration as a province in 1905, Saskatchewan was dominated by the agricultural sector. Everything revolved around wheat, from the land, the people, and what little industry made home in the province. Farmer's organizations such as the SGGA, the FUC, and later the UFC(SS) became important lobbying groups. Accordingly, the provincial Liberals, who were proponents of Free Trade and popular among the immigrant farmers, were the dominant political force up until the Great Depression. The provincial Liberals presided over a golden age of Saskatchewan growth, never to be repeated in the next century. 

The only break in Liberal dominance was the 1929 election, wherein a coalition of the CPS, PPS, and independents ousted the Liberal Party in a general opposition. Partially attributed to this oddity was the rise of the KKK in Saskatchewan, which supported Conservative leader JTM Anderson's nativist campaign. However, celebrations among the Cooperative Coalition were soon to be extinguished. 

The Great Depression hit Saskatchewan the hardest of all the provinces, especially the farmers who observed a 90% decrease in income per capita. Thousands filed into the cities looking for work only to be turned away by what little non-agricultural industry and retail existed. 

This struggle brewed a strong localized form of socialism, more impacted by British Fabianism and the Social Gospel than Marxism. From this brew sprouted the FLP, a merger of the UFPA [the political arm of the UFC(SS)] and the ILP(S), a miniscule urban socialist party whose leadership was dominated by middle class professionals rather than trade unionists. Accordingly, the early FLP was dominated by its agrarian wing and saw little electoral success in urban reas. One core policy aim of the party was the use-lease system, wherein farmers would have the option of leasing their land to the government for a guaranteed price, allowing them to pay off their debts and avoid foreclosure. Under use-lease, provincially administered Crown Land could only be leased if used for agricultural purposes. 

In the 1934 Provincial Election, the Cooperative Coalition was ousted from power, losing every single seat. Attributed to this defeat was the fall of the KKK in Saskatchewan, a subtle supporter of Anderson's nativist campaign, the collapse of the provincial Progressive Party organization, and perhaps most contributory; the start of the Great Depression. The Liberals were once again in power, opposed by only 5 FLP MLAs, all elected in rural ridings. 

The results of this election were disappointing to everybody but the LPS. The Conservatives, unbeknownst to them at the time, had seen their last major electoral success for the next 50 years. The [+ ccf_ss_name +] was also disappointed in their disappointing seat count, attributed to their presiding leader, MJ Coldwell's lack of appeal to the party's farmer base and the confusing use-lease policy which was attacked as communistic by the Liberals. Coldwell came from the ILP(S) side of the party and accordingly, Saskatchewan farmers were wholly unenthusiastic about an urban teacher leading the foremost farmers political party. Coldwell left for Federal politics in 1935, replaced by George Williams, former president of the UFC(SS) and a farmer himself.

The 1934 election indicated a brief return to the typical Liberal dominance in Saskatchewan. However, the seed of socialism had been planted and was soon to blossom. 

- @1934-1944: The CCF Blossoms
- @return: Return 

@1934-1944

In the years proceeding the 1934 election, the LPS pursued a typical Liberal-minded government. Premier Patterson extended publicly funded care for several chronic diseases, supported the establishment of Credit Unions, and generally attempted to improve labour conditions. Anderson’s Relief Program was scrapped. 

During this period, a new national party was being formed. The Co-operative Commonwealth Federation (Farmer-Labour-Socialist) was formed in 1932 from a broad movement consisting of former United Farmers Representatives, MPs of the socialist "Ginger Group", the Independent Labour Party (Manitoba), Canadian Labour Party (based in Edmonton), and the Dominion Labour Party of Southern Alberta. Also involved were intellectuals from the League for Social Reconstruction, a group of academics disillusioned with the capitalist system. 

The new Federal CCF's manifesto called for the socialization of the finance industry, state ownership of transportation, communications, and energy, free medical care to all Canadian citizens, and the abolishment of the unrepresentative Canadian Senate. The FLP would become adopted as the CCF’s provincial section but did not rebrand itself to the CCF (Saskatchewan Section) until 1936. Additionally, the [+ ccf_ss_name +] quickly removed the use-lease policy from its platform, replacing it with a vaguer promise of tenure' for farmers. 

In the capital and largest city of Regina, the CLL, an alliance of Social Democrats and Communists was swept to the power in the City Council. The new Municipal government quickly attempted to provide more comprehensive relief but was blocked by both the Federal and Provincial government. Municipalities, after all, are ‘creatures of the Provinces’ in Canada and hold no constitutional rights or responsibilities. 

Also interesting was the arrival of a new political opponent from the neighbouring province of Alberta. The Social Credit movement, based on the teachings of a British economist, advocated the distribution of "social credit" to all citizens, akin to a universal basic income to bypass large banks. In the 1935 Alberta election, Social Credit swept 56 of 63 seats. In practice, the movement became more socially conservative and its core tenent of social credit distribution was proved unconstitutional and never implemented. Nevertheless, the movement created a monstrous fervor in Western Canada, birthing the Saskatchewan Social Credit Party. 

The SCPS saw significant initial success but quickly became disorganized, resulting in Alberta Social Credit Premier Aberhart directly intervening in the party's affairs and even directing Albertan Social Credit members to aid the organization. Despite these embarrassing events, the SCPS looked poised to steal the right-wing vote from the Conservatives and the protest vote from the [+ ccf_ss_name +]. 

On the far left, the Communist Party's Saskatchewan section CP(S) was also gaining traction, especially with the rise in police-on-worker violence observed in the Regina Riot and Bienfait Coal Miner's Strike. Normally a left flank opponent of the [+ ccf_ss_name +], the CP(S) adopted a popular front strategy in the mid 30s, advocating for a coalition against the Liberals. However, very little cooperation ever occurred between the top brass of the CP(S) and the [+ ccf_ss_name +]. 

In the buildup to the 1938 provincial election, the political landscape of Saskatchewan was looking especially fractured. Limited efforts were made by all non-Liberal parties to avoid splitting the vote as parties 'sawed off' unlikely candidates. No party aside from the LPS ran a full slate of candidates. 3 "Unity" candidates as part of the Communist Popular Front strategy were nominated, all previous MLAs of the Cooperative Coalition. 

The 1938 election ended up quite turbulent. In contest were 52 seats, down from 63 in 1929 as the province underwent extreme depopulation. The new [+ ccf_ss_name +] won a significant 10 seats and the status of official opposition on top of 19% of the vote. The SCPS won only 2 seats from 16% of the vote. Most surprisingly, 2 of the Unity candidates won their seats. The Labour Progressive-Party, a front for the Communists, won 2% of the vote and no seats. The Conservatives won none as well, reduced to only 12% of the vote. And unsurprisingly, on top of all this vote splitting, the LPS won another large majority of 38 seats. 

The 1938 election was another disappointment for the [+ ccf_ss_name +]. The party had hoped to at least reduce the LPS to a minority government, but this had not been the case. George Williams' leadership was called into question though he was saved much blame due to the 4-party vote-split, allowing Williams to retain his leadership of the party. The SCPS would fare worse, completely exploding after their failed election bid. 

Patterson's returned Liberal government continued to pursue a typical Liberal agenda. A sales tax was introduced to relieve the beleaguered education system. Debt relief and the provincial pension were extended. 

The onset of World War 2 divided both the Saskatchewan and Federal CCF. William's pro-war leadership was challenged by the pacifist Carlyle King though the contest proved unsuccessful for the young King. Williams would draw much ire from the Federal CCF leader J.S. Woodsworth, a stout anti-war pacifist. 

In 1940, George Williams volunteered for the army and left the province to serve in Europe. In 1942, his leadership was contested by a young Federal MP named Tommy Douglas. Douglas was a veteran of the party, having joined from the ILP in 1930 as a protégé of Coldwell and had served as a Federal CCF MP since 1935. With Williams unable to campaign, John Brockelbank, a close Williamite ally, ran instead. However, Douglas' charisma and oratory proved too irresistible for the party base and Douglas was elected as the new leader of the [+ ccf_ss_name +]. 

Douglas focused the party on grassroots organization, expanding party membership from about 4,000 in 1939 to 24,000 by 1944. Douglas also signalled a more pragmatic approach to socialism, realizing the constraints of a socialist provincial government within the Canadian federation. 

The 1944 election would prove to be a watershed moment in Saskatchewan history. Tommy Douglas' [+ ccf_ss_name +] won a landslide victory, taking 47 of 52 seats and 53% of the vote, marking the first nominally socialist sub-national government in North America. The LPS was reduced to only 5 seats and 35% of the vote. Social Credit and the Conservatives were both decimated seat and vote wise. 

At last, it seemed, the CCF would revel in the fruits of their labour. 

- @1944-1961: The Douglas Era
- @return: Return

@1944-1961

Tommy Douglas is perhaps the most beloved politician in Canadian history. To this day, he remains regarded by many as "The Father of Medicare" and was voted "The Greatest Canadian" in a 2004 CBC poll. 

Douglas’ Administration quickly got to work at a breakneck pace. Saskatchewan General Insurance was founded in 1944 with a monopoly in automobile insurance and a mandate to expand later. SaskTel and SaskPower would expand telecommunications and power access to the province’s isolated and dispersed rural communities. Saskatchewan Transport Company was founded to provide bus transportation, bypassing Federal administration of the railways.

 In Douglas’ most personal ambition, healthcare (He was a victim of osteomyelitis), great strides were made. Universal Hospital Insurance was introduced in 1947. An air ambulance service was founded to provide swift healthcare to the province’s rural areas. The province’s healthcare system would become centralized under Health regions. 

These services would not come cheap. Joe Phelps, Minister of Resources and Development went about establishing Crown Corporations at a disorienting speed. The province expropriated a Box Factory in Prince Albert following a labour dispute. A sodium sulphate plant was acquired in 1946. The province founded new Crowns in the Wool, Leather, and Clay industries. Most of these ventures would prove unpopular, however. 

Douglas’ tenure however, also marked a period of moderation. The left flank of the [+ ccf_ss_name +] found itself weakening. In 1945, George Williams, now Minister for Agriculture died of cancer. Joe Phelps lost his seat in 1948 and the Douglas government would choose not to rerun him, believing his state-industrial endeavours had partially been responsible for reducing the Douglas government to a slim 4 seat majority. John Brockelbank would replace Phelps though by this time, he had moderated somewhat from his Williamite days and would never commit to state-industrialized planning in the same manner. 

In the poor and largely inaccessible north, dominated demographically by First Nations and Metis, the Douglas government attempted even stronger government intervention. SaskAir attempted to develop a monopoly over northern commercial flying but ultimately failed. 





